Int and Real operands can be mixed; the Int operand is converted to Real
exactly. Every other combination of sorts is an error: Booleans are not
numbers, and bit-vectors are never mixed with unbounded integers.

\strong{Division.} \code{/} is real division, as in R, so \code{x / 2} is Real even when
\code{x} is an Int. \code{\%/\%} and \code{\%\%} are Z3's integer \code{div} and \code{mod}, which
follow the SMT-LIB (Euclidean) convention: the remainder \code{a \%\% b} is
always between 0 and \code{abs(b) - 1}. This agrees with R for positive
divisors but \strong{differs for negative divisors}: in Z3 \code{7 \%/\% -2} is \code{-3}
and \code{7 \%\% -2} is \code{1}, while R gives \code{-4} and \code{-1}. Division by zero is
not an error; Z3 treats \code{x / 0} as an unspecified value.

\strong{Powers.} \code{x^k} for a literal whole number \verb{0 <= k <= 64} is expanded
into a product and keeps the sort of \code{x}. Other powers use Z3's power
operator, which is Real-valued; constraints with symbolic exponents are
often beyond Z3's decision procedures and may return \code{"unknown"}.

\strong{R values.} An R scalar combined with a Z3 expression is converted to
the sort of the other operand; see \code{\link[=z3_int_val]{z3_int_val()}} for the conversion
rules for doubles. Only length-one R values are accepted: z3r
expressions are scalars, and R's recycling rules have no meaning for a
single formula. Build several constraints with \code{\link[=lapply]{lapply()}} or \code{\link[=Map]{Map()}}.
Classed values such as dates are refused; convert them first. A
\code{bit64::integer64} works on the right of an operator (\code{x == i64}); on
the left, bit64's own operator method runs instead and fails, so write
\code{z3_int_val(ctx, i64)} there.

\strong{R's own logic.} \code{&&} and \code{||} cannot be overloaded; use \code{\link[=z3_and]{z3_and()}}
and \code{\link[=z3_or]{z3_or()}}. A Z3 expression is never converted to \code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE},
so \verb{if (x > 0)} is an error rather than a silent mistake. Note that
\code{isTRUE()} returns \code{FALSE} for any Z3 expression.
